## File Structure and Snippets

### Show Relevant Snippets, Not File Structures
Instead of defining complete file structures in your rule, focus on showing relevant code snippets that demonstrate the rule's concepts. This approach:

- **Keeps rules focused**: Shows only the code that's relevant to the rule
- **Reduces maintenance**: File structures change, but code patterns remain stable
- **Improves readability**: Easier to understand what the rule is about
- **Enables flexibility**: Developers can adapt patterns to their specific file organization

### Link to Project Structure Rule
When file organization is important to your rule, reference the project structure rule rather than duplicating it:

```markdown
## File Organization
For detailed information about project structure and file organization, see:
- [Project Structure Rule](project-structure.mdc) - Complete file organization guidelines
- [Page Patterns Rule](page-patterns.mdc) - Specific patterns for page organization
```

### Example: Good vs Bad Approach

#### ❌ Don't Define Complete File Structures
```markdown
## File Structure
```
src/
├── components/
│   ├── Button/
│   │   ├── index.tsx
│   │   ├── Button.test.tsx
│   │   └── Button.stories.tsx
├── hooks/
│   └── useButton.ts
└── types/
    └── button.ts
```
```

#### ✅ Show Relevant Snippets Instead
```markdown
## Component Structure
Components should follow this pattern:

```typescript
// Button/index.tsx
interface ButtonProps {
  readonly variant: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  readonly onClick: () => void;
}

export const Button = ({ variant, onClick }: ButtonProps) => {
  return (
    <button className={`btn btn-${variant}`} onClick={onClick}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};
```

For complete file organization guidelines, see [Project Structure Rule](project-structure.mdc).
```

